  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 310 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nAs the weekend arrives, upper ridging builds in across western \nCONUS. A weak shortwave moves out ahead of the ridge into our area \nwith gusty south southwest winds, in the range of 15 to 20 gusting \nin excess of 35 mph across the central and eastern Sandhills. A \nsurface low will be situated across western South Dakota. As \nthis low moves eastward, a cool front will move across Nebraska \nthroughout the afternoon into the evening and switching winds to\nthe northeast. Saturday highs could crest to as high as the mid\nto upper 70s before this front moves through. Whereas, Sunday \nwill be cooler with highs being slightly below normal in the mid\nto upper 60s. The air moving into our region will be quite dry \nso while the cool front would be a lifting mechanism, very \nlittle, to no precipitation is forecasted for the area at this \ntime. \n\nHeading into early next week, the Upper ridging from the West Coast \ncontinues to move across the Rockies and into the Great Plains. This \nwill promote warmer, drier conditions across much of the region. \nWhile highs Monday will favor warmer temperatures across western \nNebraska (highs in the mid to upper 70s), this will still be around \nnear normals for the season. The drier airmass could create some \ngusty winds as the air mixes to the surface. There is some relative \nagreement from guidance that areas in North Central Nebraska have at \nleast a 50 to 60 percent chance of seeing wind gusts in excess of 30 \nmph. Tuesday looks to be the peak in the warming trend where highs \nin the mid to upper 70s are likely across the region, with a \npossibility of southwest Nebraska and the eastern Panhandle to top \nout in the low 80s along and west of HWY 61.",
